;; What each tool family is for, as the prompt's tool listing says it on the
;; family's one line (discovery/format-tools-by-family, byj-gly.188). The ids
;; follow it; this is what the ids do not say. A family not named here is
;; listed with its ids alone, and an MCP server's family (`mcp$<server>`) is
;; named as that server.
;;
;; Every word here is read by the model (RFC-001): a resource, not code.
{"agent" "delegate a whole task to a specialist agent; you get back its answer"
 "agent-registry" "the subagents you dispatched and that are still alive: list them, ask one again, close one"
 "config" "read and change this project's settings, snapshot and revert them"
 "flow" "check, inspect and edit behavior-tree loops without running them"
 "hook" "code that runs on agent events: list the events, define, validate and delete hooks"
 "log" "what earlier runs did, from the event log"
 "mcp" "the connected MCP servers and what each offers"
 "memory" "what this project remembers between sessions: recall it, read it, add to it"
 "program" "measure a predictor on a dataset and propose better demos"
 "schedule" "turns of this session set to come later: a prompt on a cron or after a delay (how a /loop paces itself), or a watch on a command or file that wakes you when it changes (how to follow a long job)"
 "session" "how this project's sessions have gone: turns, cost, outcomes"
 "skills" "named multi-step procedures: find one, read it, then follow it"
 "task" "work that outlives a tool call: run it in the background, wait for it, cancel it"
 "todo" "a checklist that outlives the turn: create it, work through it, add to it"
 "tool" "define your own tools in Tool Lisp over the ones you have, test them, run one-off compositions"
 "trajectory" "export this project's sessions as datasets"}
